					<h1>Grand Prize Award Winner</h1>
					<ul>
						<li><strong>Name:</strong> Kelsie</li>
						<li><strong>Age:</strong> 13</li>
						<li><strong>Award:</strong> $5,000 to Wheelchair Sports, USA, plus Mia&#8217;s Starter Collection</li>
						<li><strong>Nominated by:</strong> Her mother, Kelli</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div id="essay">
					<h2>On track to star at the Paralympic Games</h2>
					<p>Kelsie set her sights on being on the medal stand at the Olympics early in life. Yet she was born with spina bifida and has very limited function from the waist down. She learned to walk with braces but prefers to use a wheelchair for distances. When she was 11, Kelsie met two Paralympians who introduced her to wheelchair racing&#8212;and she was hooked.</p>
					<p class="pullquote">Kelsie shows her peers that athletes with disabilities are true athletes.</p>
					<p>Kelsie set her goal to make the 2012 Paralympic team competing in London. To achieve her goal, she is committed to a rigorous training schedule. In 2007, Kelsie had spinal surgery but was back in her track chair 13 days after being released from the hospital. This year, she&#8217;s working with a personal trainer to increase strength and muscle mass. She swims with an able-bodied swim team to increase her speed and endurance. Once track season begins, she works out in her track chair and hopes to achieve her personal best each time she competes.</p>
					<p>Her family is very proud of the wonderful leader Kelsie has become through her sports experiences. She keeps flyers about disabled sports with her at all times and approaches children in wheelchairs to tell them how sports have affected her life and to encourage them to try various sports.  Kelsie helps athletes with Down syndrome prepare for the Special Olympics. She has taught able-bodied swimmers and their parents that physically challenged athletes don&#8217;t want pity but want to earn respect through commitment, hard work, and perseverance. Because of her example, she was selected to attend the Paralympic Youth Leadership Academy with 40 other emerging elite athletes.</p>
					<p class="last">Kelsie is an amazing young lady who works hard to achieve her goals and help others achieve theirs. Most importantly, Kelsie shows her peers that athletes with disabilities are true athletes.</p>
				</div>
